 particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements II and III are false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/assets/fabbbb78d8fd4d327fe82d1d93d8e0fc15dd33e909266e0ef88de931be28b951.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- Statement I is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves **clockwise** along $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/assets/712564e3b0ad0e23d2d9097eb02c0857130bdaa6619a005738205130ab66ac3a.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-125643/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f30993e-b5c7-5d8a-9b27-2aed2c9223e1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-125643"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-125395"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/assets/0ae64bf57444ac42ec5efed392471cbbac7b1e317bc1fb06b33bcd2038a2e3c1.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n- ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I and II are false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves clockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/assets/bc0121782b16d513d7d17d3741bd07ef1716e096bb2459fbda0cb841c7fd8b43.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves **clockwise** along $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/assets/1a6b109bc1da3aa7905b05f7f7902523b45fd51a65b97bec1328037bd6c0cfe7.png)\n\nSo, ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-125585/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "90d86725-c631-527d-bfaa-25e158c8f879" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125585/selection/8b0da25323387be7cd3dda381ee4e2ebbdc8b10be241bc2e3eeead0da24f888a" :answer/id #uuid "ca042d97-9382-5757-9565-f4c9a592ef0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125585/selection/ad963bcdf2fe034ec60767210e5b564fc417b3b8813d0edc4c20ea2f1c8c2358" :answer/id #uuid "579300e3-dcc6-534e-a548-566e75171ca5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125585/selection/1e92e7e13074920ac5aa882943c0d0cc16dd496cdd41b0ca0e7d7d1595497ada" :answer/id #uuid "66b3b284-8ce7-592d-ad66-af8680d66ca4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125585/selection/b9e37961d17591920349894dfae8783436f581130d0da0bd042142640bfd4f67" :answer/id #uuid "87d55e48-85da-575a-8f45-e51f73e826fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125585/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ea096e98-8fd9-55ff-82e5-2cb9154735dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-125585/selection/ad963bcdf2fe034ec60767210e5b564fc417b3b8813d0edc4c20ea2f1c8c2358" :answer-field/choices ["q-125585/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-125585/selection/8b0da25323387be7cd3dda381ee4e2ebbdc8b10be241bc2e3eeead0da24f888a" "q-125585/selection/ad963bcdf2fe034ec60767210e5b564fc417b3b8813d0edc4c20ea2f1c8c2358" "q-125585/selection/1e92e7e13074920ac5aa882943c0d0cc16dd496cdd41b0ca0e7d7d1595497ada" "q-125585/selection/b9e37961d17591920349894dfae8783436f581130d0da0bd042142640bfd4f67"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-125585" :question/id #uuid "8708f396-8f97-549d-8100-2f26c28b54d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-125585" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{3}{y}\\,i-\\frac{2}{x}\\,j$ along the ellipse $C$ with equation $9{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=36$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $2$ and $3,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{3}{y}\\,i-\\frac{2}{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-2-3 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-5)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-5t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-5(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =-10\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-125585/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-125585"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-125455/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "ac8e19d8-a2d7-5a7a-aeb7-46b0a57e7c70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125455/selection/6813ab1041b0710ec600e525b5dde1529e9cdde2099f23754ba54ec5d845bb88" :answer/id #uuid "375f4654-10d8-597d-9e0e-6fc2c7bb5823" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125455/selection/1e92e7e13074920ac5aa882943c0d0cc16dd496cdd41b0ca0e7d7d1595497ada" :answer/id #uuid "5d04edb5-4073-5319-b6b4-97d83f481809" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125455/selection/01bbf8ebd5069470431f8452e98a426e151975cb8793ee4f306144a87060a9a3" :answer/id #uuid "472c2a6d-e736-5dc6-95af-d3d143da495a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125455/selection/8b0da25323387be7cd3dda381ee4e2ebbdc8b10be241bc2e3eeead0da24f888a" :answer/id #uuid "37c240a4-9b13-5a2d-9a26-c48541a3423d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125455/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "72cbbcd4-9b0c-515d-acb3-e83a7f1948f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-125455/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-125455/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-125455/selection/6813ab1041b0710ec600e525b5dde1529e9cdde2099f23754ba54ec5d845bb88" "q-125455/selection/1e92e7e13074920ac5aa882943c0d0cc16dd496cdd41b0ca0e7d7d1595497ada" "q-125455/selection/01bbf8ebd5069470431f8452e98a426e151975cb8793ee4f306144a87060a9a3" "q-125455/selection/8b0da25323387be7cd3dda381ee4e2ebbdc8b10be241bc2e3eeead0da24f888a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-125455" :question/id #uuid "b2759970-7296-5ed9-9b76-591bf564c402" :question/math-academy-id "q-125455" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =x\\,i+y\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}(\\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}(-1+1) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-125455/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-125455"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-125457/selection/6813ab1041b0710ec600e525b5dde1529e9cdde2099f23754ba54ec5d845bb88" :answer/id #uuid "dde6421c-a0ec-5264-8c92-9b0a637dca86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125457/selection/cd8957b3e03d9fbea87893281373eded912abde9aae6462917a9ce8659b8cc99" :answer/id #uuid "60d076cb-e78c-5ba9-891b-6fbb24e6b378" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125457/selection/d62732e72e17b0393290bd9e548ef368f6b7424bded817d038b07db5bf5fcb98" :answer/id #uuid "03f084cc-0963-58a0-b760-90872dd423c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125457/selection/58c8224fa0585dda2549ac030dde71d2e44791b603a950cf32ce3e2d4ed2296d" :answer/id #uuid "a3d655fc-fecd-566e-a112-395d1edf56c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-15\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125457/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "a1bb5fe0-c4b7-539a-b5e3-526ba94a91ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125457/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "88e21619-c39f-5b4c-a291-814dec44aada" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-125457/selection/6813ab1041b0710ec600e525b5dde1529e9cdde2099f23754ba54ec5d845bb88" :answer-field/choices ["q-125457/selection/6813ab1041b0710ec600e525b5dde1529e9cdde2099f23754ba54ec5d845bb88" "q-125457/selection/cd8957b3e03d9fbea87893281373eded912abde9aae6462917a9ce8659b8cc99" "q-125457/selection/d62732e72e17b0393290bd9e548ef368f6b7424bded817d038b07db5bf5fcb98" "q-125457/selection/58c8224fa0585dda2549ac030dde71d2e44791b603a950cf32ce3e2d4ed2296d" "q-125457/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-125457" :question/id #uuid "392b0319-d12c-58e9-a1f7-7742e3726555" :question/math-academy-id "q-125457" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\,i-\\frac{1}{x}\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{y}\\,i-\\frac{1}{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-2(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =-4\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-125457/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-125457"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-125451/selection/7df0c696b34bad288a681ae029960fe34e17b9ede842edd8931820e47f716610" :answer/id #uuid "517b42aa-a18c-55bb-a13d-1df1be4b547b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125451/selection/240ec3d8434c02553987bfbb2f37ed146250c7b9acb5a5e9aab45f367730d44d" :answer/id #uuid "c52f743d-35be-56a2-9a85-c1ec7c595820" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125451/selection/d0ab94490dee12664d2f70471811316d5faf74c9c2c3f6dde546976ea292b76b" :answer/id #uuid "86336e09-0e45-5c76-ab74-aa242d8d2f29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125451/selection/681b79a6d9c8561f20779e64cdd4e65c04e644d384b1c125c96c6be6072de751" :answer/id #uuid "5c282543-d2dd-5b79-9c98-8e85e4ba446d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125451/selection/a996cecead059c2e05b8ded2ceb60e4e2170a4344e0c20625524ed13b23c6fbe" :answer/id #uuid "5eb06a4d-27c2-5262-a18a-da49c3612320" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125451/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7818b6b2-6228-58cb-b0a2-d6bb230dc743" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-125451/selection/681b79a6d9c8561f20779e64cdd4e65c04e644d384b1c125c96c6be6072de751" :answer-field/choices ["q-125451/selection/7df0c696b34bad288a681ae029960fe34e17b9ede842edd8931820e47f716610" "q-125451/selection/240ec3d8434c02553987bfbb2f37ed146250c7b9acb5a5e9aab45f367730d44d" "q-125451/selection/d0ab94490dee12664d2f70471811316d5faf74c9c2c3f6dde546976ea292b76b" "q-125451/selection/681b79a6d9c8561f20779e64cdd4e65c04e644d384b1c125c96c6be6072de751" "q-125451/selection/a996cecead059c2e05b8ded2ceb60e4e2170a4344e0c20625524ed13b23c6fbe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-125451" :question/id #uuid "f2471c91-31ed-50a8-8d79-11eaace5c1e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-125451" :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=y\\,i-x\\,j$ along the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =y\\,i-x\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\sqrt{3}{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(-\\sqrt{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-125451/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "976e05d4-59a8-5a93-be2f-b22357f388b9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-125451"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-125485"] :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $Γ$ of the vector field $F(x,y)=\\frac{5}{y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j$ along the ellipse $C$ with equation $25{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=25$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation of our curve as\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThe closed curve $C$ is the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal and vertical semiaxes $1$ and $5,$ respectively. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t)) & =\\frac{5}{y}\\,i+\\frac{1}{x}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-1+5 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4t\\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =4(2\\pi )-0 \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-125647/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "309af1fa-2d22-5ba1-8347-54f0753cd709" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125647/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "353a6d5f-e9e2-53f4-aaac-16a831a2db6b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125647/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "278dcc01-779f-58fb-937f-da6619bd0d19" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125647/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "aec496a2-8ac1-53ef-a9e1-7cb7f99f8eb3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125647/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "fa3818b8-a9b4-54b9-9d2b-8000f8cf8fd3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125647/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "66e25685-0092-5600-bb01-d03dec7a05f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-125647/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-125647/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-125647/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-125647/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-125647/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-125647/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-125647" :question/id #uuid "5698feb4-caca-57b7-b731-3c38dfa0d952" :question/math-academy-id "q-125647" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $∇f$ is conservative\n- ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- ${∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $∇f$ is conservative by definition.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-125647/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da220a9-2afc-5b63-a423-a4d2b3de9567"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-125647"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-125648/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "a2aab56f-b319-52a9-ae32-1e81456c4207" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125648/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "5c8f3f34-0871-53be-8c01-6cff9e6c6c41" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125648/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "c097e4b3-36d0-59ab-9fc6-e46b14688bc1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125648/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "ae5a73ba-3ca1-5282-bcfc-1c474448a553" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125648/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "b0f19df0-fbfc-57e4-aa1c-6d64d2d3c52c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125648/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7c72ee41-ee80-550b-ac6b-9e9b7b61e02b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-125648/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-125648/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-125648/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-125648/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-125648/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-125648/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-125648" :question/id #uuid "68da615c-63bd-5ae0-ad3e-17f88a638f80" :question/math-academy-id "q-125648" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)={e}^{y}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $∇f$ is **not** conservative\n- ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any piecewise linear path $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- ${∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any circle $C\\in {R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The vector field $∇f$ is conservative by definition.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-125648/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da220a9-2afc-5b63-a423-a4d2b3de9567"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-125648"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-125424"] :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $∇f$ is conservative\n- ${∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any piecewise smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $∇f$ is conservative by definition.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-125664/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "a31525d9-fd0a-5f3d-884f-d6254cfa35c2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125664/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" :answer/id #uuid "7a5f0726-4897-5532-acbc-7f2bd9c03f2a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125664/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "deb637da-a986-53da-8795-0fafd7c206f2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125664/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "fd222e95-2a78-5911-86b7-a9db8fdbe8ba" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125664/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "1cb12ce2-b7c0-5b90-9e9e-5f012568424b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125664/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c914888b-9af6-554b-ae9b-58d34794b8a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-125664/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer-field/choices ["q-125664/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-125664/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" "q-125664/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-125664/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-125664/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-125664" :question/id #uuid "b51b0bd3-4412-5063-a01c-3d1c473d5dbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-125664" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=({y}^{2}+3x)\\,i+(2xy+3)\\,j.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is conservative\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any circle $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement II and III are true. The circulation $Γ=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-125664/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da220a9-2afc-5b63-a423-a4d2b3de9567"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-125664"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-125650/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "537c23ef-2363-5af6-ba72-684db01fc37f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125650/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "067bc495-58c8-5450-83ae-1b7fa73ef9fc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125650/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" :answer/id #uuid "a0243bd2-afa7-55c5-88bc-0907a126fbd4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125650/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "c249bf83-752d-5790-834b-e18b49a87ff8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125650/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "dc1af504-1c71-52fc-af06-69994b47edde" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-125650/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "443f5a5a-cca8-5554-9dac-8213da64ded0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-125650/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer-field/choices ["q-125650/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-125650/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-125650/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" "q-125650/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-125650/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-125650" :question/id #uuid "37b7b2b9-7471-5e99-bc34-8505b7073f9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-125650" :question/problem "Consider the scalar field $f(x,y)=x{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+y.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $∇f$ is conservative\n- ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- ${∮}_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r\\ne 0$ for some closed paths $C\\in {R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $∇f$ is conservative by definition.\n\n- Statement II is false. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\n- Statement III is false. The circulation $Γ=0$ for any closed path $C\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-125650/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da220a9-2afc-5b63-a423-a4d2b3de9567"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-125650"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9537"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/assets/f7e61f72ed7488fd490f42ff519316ed2e87d3c997d64e2cc6b7af36f951dbb5.png)\n\nConsider the circle $S$ and vector field $\\mathbf F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $S$ is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =  0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r\n= \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r <                               0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. Since the force $\\mathbf F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves around $C,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/assets/063836a4780ecb4be6e8c6149a422e3c8e9f2770304e659a8116691b91e3d7f1.png)\n\nSo, $\\displaystyle\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $\\mathbf F$ remains unchanged.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060266/assets/704915d2852e1654aa31d17bd2dfb07a3022fe3d8b597f37bf98bd5af2de1d1b.png)\n\nSo, $\\displaystyle\\oint_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9539"] :question/problem "Calculate the circulation $\\Gamma$ of the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) = -y\\,\\mathbf{i} +x\\,\\mathbf{j}$ along the circle $C$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 = 1,$ oriented counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The closed curve $C$ is the unit circle. It can be parameterized in the counterclockwise direction as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\cos{t}\\,\\mathbf{i} + \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf{j},\n\\qquad 0 \\leq t \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe circulation $\\Gamma$ measures the total work done by a force $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r\n= \\int_a^b \\mathbf F(r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) \\; \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx = \\cos t, \\qquad\ny = \\sin t.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))=-y\\,i+x\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf r'(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9543"] :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y) =( e^y+x)\\,\\mathbf i +(xe^y+y^2)\\,\\mathbf j.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf F$ is conservative\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r = 0$ for any piecewise-smooth path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r =0$ for any closed path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2$" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf F$ is a conservative vector field on $\\mathbb R^2,$ then the circulation $\\Gamma = 0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in\\mathbb R^2,$ that is \n\n$$\n\\Gamma = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm{d} \\mathbf r = 0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field with domain $D = \\mathbb R^2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} =  e^y.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The vector field $\\mathbf F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. The result $\\Gamma = 0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
